The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2314 (2016), and 2319 (2016),

Condemning in the strongest terms the reported use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, in particular the attack on Khan Shaykhun reported on 4 April 2017, which resulted in horrifying loss of life and injuries to civilians, including many children,

Expressing its outrage that individuals continue to be killed and injured by chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, and expressing its determination that those responsible must be held accountable,

Recalling that in resolution 2118 (2013) it decided that the Syrian Arab Republic shall not use, develop, produce, otherwise acquire, stockpile or retain chemical weapons, or transfer, directly or indirectly, chemical weapons to other States or non-State actors, and further decided that the Syrian Arab Republic shall cooperate fully with the OPCW and the United Nations,

Reaffirming that the use of chemical weapons anywhere constitutes a grave violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,

Welcoming the commencement of the investigation by the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) Fact-Finding Mission into the incident in Khan Shaykhun, and reaffirming its full support for the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission and the OPCW-United Nations Joint Investigative Mechanism,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the reported use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, in particular the attack on Khan Shaykhun in Idlib Governorate on 4 April 2017;

2. Expresses its full support to the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission and the OPCW-United Nations Joint Investigative Mechanism, and requests the Fact-Finding Mission to report on the results of its investigation into the Khan Shaykhun incident as expeditiously as possible;

3. Demands that all parties in the Syrian Arab Republic provide the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission and the OPCW-United Nations Joint Investigative Mechanism with full, safe, and unimpeded access to any relevant sites, individuals, and material;

4. Demands that the Syrian Arab Republic, in accordance with its obligations under resolution 2118 (2013) and the Chemical Weapons Convention, comply with the following requirements immediately:
(a) Provide full flight plans, flight logs, and any other information on air operations conducted on 4 April 2017, including details of all aircraft and helicopter sorties;
(b) Provide the names of all individuals in command of any aircraft squadrons operating on 4 April 2017 and make such individuals available immediately for interview by investigators;
(c) Grant immediate and unhindered access to relevant airbases from which attacks involving chemical weapons may have been launched, including the Shayrat airbase;

5. Requests the Director-General of the OPCW and the Secretary-General of the United Nations to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution within thirty days;

6. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

